  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reducing Slack in Informal Transit: The Impact of Temporary Subsidies on Urban Mobility</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This study investigates how targeted regulatory and financial interventions can improve urban mobility in developing cities by reducing inefficiencies in informal, privately provided shared transport (minibus taxis). Specifically, we test the long-term impact of temporary subsidies designed to increase departure frequencies and establish new routes, alongside a pilot rotation system using tradable, alternate-day work tickets to mitigate wasteful vehicle queueing. Ultimately, we measure whether these short-term policy interventions can break up local monopolies and deliver lasting improvements to urban transit access.</narrative>
  </description>
